 Industrial monitoring covers tasks such as fault detection, checking a system's physical condition,
 assessing manufacturing quality, and confirming that a robot has completed its task. The traditional
 approach uses single sensor readings and engineered features to characterise the system's behaviour.
-Learned representations take this further by utilising data from images, sound and sensor sequences.
+Learned representations take this further by using data from images, sound and sensor sequences.
 Multimodal learning examines how information from different modalities can be combined to support a
 common prediction task \parencite{Baltrusaitis2019Multimodal,Huo2022Multisensor,Thoduka2024Handover}.
 
 The literature touches on several related areas of research: traditional feature engineering, the
-use of pre-trained representations, modelling over time, feature combination, prediction fusion, and
+use of pretrained representations, modelling over time, feature combination, prediction fusion, and
 adaptive methods (where the method can adapt to the context). They differ in the information they
 require, their computational cost, the level of supervision they need, and their sensitivity to
 input quality.
 
 Industrial and robotic monitoring provides a platform on which to compare these approaches as the
 process can be monitored via a number of different modalities (cameras, acoustic sensors, machine
-sensors, etc.). Cameras will obviously provide a view of the visible process, whereas the acoustic
+sensors, etc.). Cameras provide a view of the visible process, whereas the acoustic
 and machine sensors provide information which is not visible. The benefit of combining data depends
 heavily on what each modality retains, the specific prediction you're trying to make, and the
 system's test conditions \parencite{Wang2020What,Hua2026Weld,Goodarzi2023Deep}. Simply increasing the
 amount of data does not necessarily lead to better predictions.
 
-Work on welding using visual and acoustic data has shown that multimodal integration can improve prediction performance, but also that, in some cases, adding a further modality provides no benefit at all \parencite{P003,Hua2026Weld}. These findings raise a more general architectural question across industrial and robotic monitoring tasks. Under what conditions does a second observation supply information about system condition or task outcome that the strongest single input misses?
+Work on welding using visual and acoustic data has shown that multimodal integration can improve prediction performance, but also that, in some cases, adding another modality provides no benefit at all \parencite{P003,Hua2026Weld}. These findings raise a more general architectural question across industrial and robotic monitoring tasks. Under what conditions does a second observation supply information about system condition or task outcome that the strongest single input misses?
 
